## Why This Is Asked
Real-world Android apps blend local caching with network fetches, testing understanding of data-source layering and MVVM architecture fundamentals.

## Key Concepts
- **Room**: Entities, DAOs, and database initialization
- **Retrofit**: REST API client with JSON parsing
- **Repository Pattern**: Offline-first strategy with cache fallback
- **ViewModel + StateFlow/LiveData**: UI state management
- **Swipe-to-Refresh**: Pull-to-refresh pattern for manual data refresh
- **Network Connectivity Checks**: Determining when to fetch versus use cache

## Code Example
```kotlin
@Entity(tableName = "articles")
data class Article(
    @PrimaryKey val id: Int,
    val title: String,
    val summary: String,
    val updatedAt: Long
)

@Dao
interface ArticleDao {
    @Query("SELECT * FROM articles ORDER BY updatedAt DESC")
    fun getAll(): Flow<List<Article>>

    @Insert(onConflict = OnConflictStrategy.REPLACE)
    suspend fun upsertAll(articles: List<Article>)
}

class ArticleRepository(
    private val dao: ArticleDao,
    private val api: NewsApi
) {
    fun getArticles(): Flow<Result<List<Article>>> = flow {
        dao.getAll().collect { cached ->
            emit(Result.success(cached))
            try {
                val network = api.getArticles()
                dao.upsertAll(network)
            } catch (e: Exception) {
                // Fall back to cached data already emitted
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Offline Behavior**: Enable airplane mode, launch app, verify cached articles display
2. **Cache Loading**: Clear app data, launch, verify initial JSON preload renders
3. **Refresh Flow**: Enable network, swipe to refresh, verify UI updates with fresh data
4. **Error Handling**: Mock network failure, verify graceful fallback to cached content

## RecyclerView Implementation

- Create item layout XML with TextViews for name and email
- Extend RecyclerView.Adapter with custom ViewHolder class
- Override three required methods: onCreateViewHolder, onBindViewHolder, getItemCount
- Use ViewHolder pattern to avoid repeated findViewById calls
- Set adapter and LayoutManager on RecyclerView in Activity/Fragment

## Key Components

- **ViewHolder**: Holds references to view components
- **Adapter**: Bridges data with RecyclerView
- **LayoutManager**: Positions items (LinearLayoutManager for lists)
- **Data Binding**: Optional but recommended for cleaner code

```kotlin
class UserAdapter(private val users: List<User>) : 
    RecyclerView.Adapter<UserAdapter.UserViewHolder>() {

    class UserViewHolder(view: View) : RecyclerView.ViewHolder(view) {
        val nameText: TextView = view.findViewById(R.id.name)
        val emailText: TextView = view.findViewById(R.id.email)
    }

    override fun onCreateViewHolder(parent: ViewGroup, viewType: Int): UserViewHolder {
        val view = LayoutInflater.from(parent.context)
            .inflate(R.layout.item_user, parent, false)
        return UserViewHolder(view)
    }

    override fun onBindViewHolder(holder: UserViewHolder, position: Int) {
        val user = users[position]
        holder.nameText.text = user.name
        holder.emailText.text = user.email
    }

    override fun getItemCount() = users.size
}
```

